                <pb facs="0672" n="660" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg660"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.796/213: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-15T12:00:00-03:00">August 15,
                        1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 1:29 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>406. Department’s instruction No. 801, July 30. At this juncture the Brazilian
                    Government has paramount interest in financial aid from us for three purposes:
                    Acquiring armament in the United States; building the steel mill;<anchor n="87"
                        xml:id="d785-f1"/> and preventing the collapse of their exchange situation
                    (the exchange situation is daily becoming weaker) and it would be excessively
                    difficult to capture their attention now for projects of the sort described in
                    the Department’s instruction No. 801.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
